      {
        "src": "OEBPS/assets/image-1-300x174.png",
        "alt": "The basic process of Action Research is as follows: Plan a change; Take action to enact the change; Observe the process and consequences of the change; Reflect on the process and consequences; Act, observe, & reflect again and so on.",
        "role": null,
        "html": "<img xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml\" class=\"alignleft wp-image-158\" src=\"assets/image-1-300x174.png\" alt=\"The basic process of Action Research is as follows: Plan a change; Take action to enact the change; Observe the process and consequences of the change; Reflect on the process and consequences; Act, observe, &amp; reflect again and so on.\" width=\"517\" height=\"300\" />",
        "location": "OEBPS/chapter-001-chapt1.html#epubcfi(/4/2%5Bchapt1%5D/4/2/2/32/2)"
      },
      {
        "src": "OEBPS/assets/McKemmis-Taggart-300x161.png",
        "alt": "Kemmis and McTaggart (2004) offer a slightly different process for action research: Plan; Act & Observe; Reflect; Revised Plan; Act & Observe; Reflect.",
        "role": null,
        "html": "<img xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml\" class=\"alignnone wp-image-69\" src=\"assets/McKemmis-Taggart-300x161.png\" alt=\"Kemmis and McTaggart (2004) offer a slightly different process for action research: Plan; Act &amp; Observe; Reflect; Revised Plan; Act &amp; Observe; Reflect.\" width=\"821\" height=\"441\" />",
        "location": "OEBPS/chapter-001-chapt1.html#epubcfi(/4/2%5Bchapt1%5D/4/2/24/6/2)"
      },
      {
        "src": "OEBPS/assets/Oleary-1-265x300.png",
        "alt": "O'Leary (2004) offers another version of the action research process that focuses the cyclical nature of action research, with three cycles shown: Observe; Reflect; Plan; Act; And Repeat.",
        "role": null,
        "html": "<img xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml\" class=\"alignnone wp-image-71\" src=\"assets/Oleary-1-265x300.png\" alt=\"O'Leary (2004) offers another version of the action research process that focuses the cyclical nature of action research, with three cycles shown: Observe; Reflect; Plan; Act; And Repeat.\" width=\"734\" height=\"831\" />",
        "location": "OEBPS/chapter-001-chapt1.html#epubcfi(/4/2%5Bchapt1%5D/4/2/24/12/2)"
      },
      {
        "src": "OEBPS/assets/MacIntyre-300x258.png",
        "alt": "Macintyre (2000) offers a much more complex process of action research that highlights multiple processes happening at the same time. It starts with: Reflection and analysis of current practice and general idea of research topic and context. Second: Narrowing down the topic, planning the action; and scanning the literature, discussing with colleagues. Third: Refined topic – selection of key texts, formulation of research question/hypothesis, organization of refined action plan in context; and tentative action plan, consideration of different research strategies. Fourth: Evaluation of entire process; and take action, monitor effects – evaluation of strategy and research question/hypothesis and final amendments. Lastly: Conclusions, claims, explanations. Recommendations for further research.",
        "role": null,
        "html": "<img xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml\" class=\"alignnone wp-image-72\" src=\"assets/MacIntyre-300x258.png\" alt=\"Macintyre (2000) offers a much more complex process of action research that highlights multiple processes happening at the same time. It starts with: Reflection and analysis of current practice and general idea of research topic and context. Second: Narrowing down the topic, planning the action; and scanning the literature, discussing with colleagues. Third: Refined topic – selection of key texts, formulation of research question/hypothesis, organization of refined action plan in context; and tentative action plan, consideration of different research strategies. Fourth: Evaluation of entire process; and take action, monitor effects – evaluation of strategy and research question/hypothesis and final amendments. Lastly: Conclusions, claims, explanations. Recommendations for further research.\" width=\"783\" height=\"673\" />",
        "location": "OEBPS/chapter-001-chapt1.html#epubcfi(/4/2%5Bchapt1%5D/4/2/24/18/2)"
      },
      {
        "src": "OEBPS/assets/Figure3_1-300x169.jpg",
        "alt": "O'Leary (2004) provides a flow chart for reviewing the literature. He identifies four action categories for reviewing literature: Find it; Manage it; Use it; Review it. Finding it includes: Knowing the literature types; Using available resources; Honing your search skills. Managing it includes: Reading efficiently; Keeping track of references; Writing relevant annotations. Using it includes: Choosing your research topic; Developing your question; Arguing your rationale; Informing your study with theory; Designing method. Reviewing it includes: Understanding the lit review's purpose; Ensuring adequate coverage; Writing purposefully; Working on style and tone.",
        "role": null,
        "html": "<img xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml\" class=\"wp-image-36\" src=\"assets/Figure3_1-300x169.jpg\" alt=\"O'Leary (2004) provides a flow chart for reviewing the literature. He identifies four action categories for reviewing literature: Find it; Manage it; Use it; Review it. Finding it includes: Knowing the literature types; Using available resources; Honing your search skills. Managing it includes: Reading efficiently; Keeping track of references; Writing relevant annotations. Using it includes: Choosing your research topic; Developing your question; Arguing your rationale; Informing your study with theory; Designing method. Reviewing it includes: Understanding the lit review's purpose; Ensuring adequate coverage; Writing purposefully; Working on style and tone.\" width=\"892\" height=\"502\" />",
        "location": "OEBPS/chapter-003-chapt3.html#epubcfi(/4/2%5Bchapt3%5D/4/2/106%5Battachment_36%5D/2)"
      },
      {
        "src": "OEBPS/assets/Picture1-300x47.png",
        "alt": "Cresswell (2009) provides a framework for data analysis and interpretation: Organize and prepare raw data for analysis; Read and engage with all data; Code data; Determine themes, codes, and descriptors; Interrelate themes, codes, and description; Interpret meaning.",
        "role": null,
        "html": "<img xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml\" class=\"alignnone wp-image-104\" src=\"assets/Picture1-300x47.png\" alt=\"Cresswell (2009) provides a framework for data analysis and interpretation: Organize and prepare raw data for analysis; Read and engage with all data; Code data; Determine themes, codes, and descriptors; Interrelate themes, codes, and description; Interpret meaning.\" width=\"811\" height=\"127\" />",
        "location": "OEBPS/chapter-006-chap6.html#epubcfi(/4/2%5Bchap6%5D/4/2/52/2)"
      }
    ]
